{"tool_name":"cagedb","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"cagedb","home":"http://cagedb.uhlenlab.org/","summary":"Mapping genes for calcium signaling and their associated human genetic disorders.","addition_date":"2018-06-07T15:22:00Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-25T14:39:20.745125Z","tool_type":["Database portal"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:cagedb","credit":[{"email":"per.uhlen@ki.se","name":"Per Uhl\u00e9n","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://cagedb.uhlenlab.org/documentation.html"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Database search","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2421"}]}],"name":"The Ca2+ genome database","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"d.gabrielaitis","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btx225","metadata":{"abstract":"Motivation: Signal transduction via calcium ions (Ca2+) represents a fundamental signaling pathway in all eukaryotic cells. A large portion of the human genome encodes proteins used to assemble signaling systems that can transduce signals with diverse spatial and temporal dynamics. Results: Here, we provide a map of all of the genes involved in Ca2+ signaling and link these genes to human genetic disorders. Using Gene Ontology terms and genome databases, 1805 genes were identified as regulators or targets of intracellular Ca2+ signals. Associating these 1805 genes with human genetic disorders uncovered 1470 diseases with mutated 'Ca2+ genes'. A network with scale-free properties appeared when the Ca2+ genes were mapped to their associated genetic disorders.","authors":[{"name":"Arenas E."},{"name":"Hortenhuber M."},{"name":"Louhivuori L."},{"name":"Malmersjo S."},{"name":"Smedler E."},{"name":"Toledo E.M."},{"name":"Uhlen P."}],"citationCount":17,"date":"2017-08-15T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"Mapping genes for calcium signaling and their